---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { src } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Solo Tax Desk 2026: who builds the 1099 and LLC calculators';
const description = 'Solo Tax Desk is published by Radif Partners: 2026 self-employment, S corp and LLC calculators built on IRS and state documents. No email asked, nothing sold.';
const verified = K.states.filter((s) => s.llc.formation !== null).length;
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Solo Tax Desk</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why the site was built</h2>
    <p>Search "llc vs s corp" and the first page of Google is a row of articles: the U.S. Chamber of Commerce, Wolters Kluwer, Stripe, LegalShield. None of them computes anything. Search for an S corp savings calculator and the tools belong to payroll companies and accounting firms (Gusto, Collective, Keeper, several CPA practices) that sell the election, the payroll or the bookkeeping that comes after it; Gusto's own page says it leaves out the QBI deduction and state and city taxes. One popular two-field LLC calculator has no tax year and no state at all, and one 1099 widget still displayed "20XX".</p>
    <p>This site does better than the first Google results for these searches because it gives the 2026 figure at once, without an email: self-employment tax, federal income tax after the QBI deduction, the S corp saving after payroll costs, and for each state the LLC fees, the franchise or minimum tax, the income tax and the city taxes on business profit, every number linked to the IRS, SSA or state page it was read on.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>{AUTHOR_NAME} is a company that builds calculators on public rules. It is not a CPA firm, a tax preparer, a payroll provider or a company-formation service, it has no partner to send you to, and it earns nothing when you form an LLC or elect S status. No page links to a formation service.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What the numbers rest on</h2>
    <p>Federal figures come from <Fragment set:html={src('rp_2025_32', 'Rev. Proc. 2025-32')} /> (brackets, standard deduction, QBI thresholds), the <Fragment set:html={src('f1040es', '2026 Form 1040-ES package')} /> (the {K.usd(K.P.se.wageBase['2026'])} Social Security limit, the estimated tax rules and dates), <Fragment set:html={src('p15', 'Publication 15')} /> (payroll taxes) and <Fragment set:html={src('ssa_2027', 'the SSA fact sheet of October 2026')} />. State figures were read on each Secretary of State and revenue department site: {verified} states and DC have their LLC fees verified today. A figure we could not read on an official page is shown as "n/a", never estimated.</p>
    <h2>What it cannot do</h2>
    <p>The calculators see only what you type. They do not know your credits, your other investments, a loss carried from last year or a special rule of your city. They give an estimate of your 2026 taxes and the reasoning behind it, not advice for your business. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every assumption, and the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> says how errors are fixed.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>A wrong figure, a newer state notice, a broken source link: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. We cannot answer questions about your own return.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
